{"t":12.7848,"ev":{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"f5df5f7b-31b0-41e8-a16d-bc7683471e6e","status":"SUCCESS","response":"Lighthouses date back to ancient civilizations, where early mariners were guided by bonfires lit atop coastal hills and stone beacons. The most renowned ancient example, the Pharos of Alexandria, stood as a marvel of engineering in the third century BCE. Over the centuries, these rudimentary structures evolved into fortified stone towers designed to withstand ferocious coastal storms and mark perilous reefs.\n\nAt their core, modern lighthouses operate by magnifying and projecting concentrated beams of light across long oceanic distances. The invention of the Fresnel lens in the early 19th century revolutionized this process, capturing scattered rays through stepped concentric rings to produce powerful, highly visible beams. Each lighthouse flashes a distinct pattern, or light signature, allowing navigators to identify their exact geographic position in the dark.\n\nAlthough modern vessels rely heavily on GPS and electronic navigation, lighthouses remain indispensable aids to navigation. Digital systems can suffer from signal loss, power failure, or cyber interference, leaving physical visual markers as a vital failsafe. Beyond their enduring safety role, lighthouses stand as historic monuments celebrating human ingenuity and maritime heritage.\n","duration_seconds":5.417375,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":11945,"output_tokens":219,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":0,"total_tokens":12164}}}}
